    "created_at": "2016-11-02T12:00:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-11-02T12:00:52Z",
    "body": "/CC @cpearce @jernoble\n\nI wrote this simple test page that shows how the `preload` IDL attribute behaves: https://mounirlamouri.github.io/sandbox/preload/api-exposure.html\n\nAs far as I can see:\n- Gecko shows the \"parsed\" `preload` and the empty string if unknown/invalid.\n- WebKit shows the \"parsed\" `preload` and `auto` if unknown/invalid even on iOS where `metadata` is the default one and `auto` is not allowed.\n- Blink shows the `effective preload state`. It would behave like Firefox and Safari when all values are allowed apart for unknown/invalid cases. When unknown/invalid reverts to `auto`, Chrome and Safari will behave the same.\n\nUA seems to have very different behaviour. I think it would unlikely break websites to change the spec to ask `preload` to expose the `effective preload type` but because it would not affect Blink, I might be biased :).\n\n@cpearce and @jernoble, WDYT?\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
